<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakr al-Siddiq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 573–<a href="/years/634">634 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Bakr al-Siddiq was a close companion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, ruling from 632 to 634. He suppressed the revolts known as the Ridda wars, reuniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> under Muslim rule. He also launched the first campaigns into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> that began the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquests</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-al-siddiq.jpg" alt="Abu Bakr al-Siddiq" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Ahsmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20131203_Istanbul_091.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Saqifah of the Banu Sa’ida, a covered hall in Medina, leading companions debated the succession within hours of the Prophet’s death and acclaimed <b>Abu Bakr al-Siddiq</b>, Muhammad’s close companion and father-in-law, as the first caliph, from the Arabic khalifa, “successor” or “deputy”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, son of a Companion of the Prophet and grandson of <b>Abu Bakr</b>, refused to recognise the Umayyad caliph Yazid I and took refuge in Mecca.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li><li><blockquote>When Muhammad died in 632, <b>Abu Bakr</b> was chosen as his successor, founding the Rashidun Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-state-of-medina">Islamic state of Medina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=612" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=612">What was here in 612 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
